    pearls: "The \"triple whammy\" of NSAID + ACEi/ARB + diuretic (or volume depletion) is the most common drug-induced AKI combination in elderly patients. NSAIDs inhibit prostaglandin-mediated afferent arteriolar vasodilation, while ACEi inhibit angiotensin II-mediated efferent arteriolar constriction — together they drastically reduce GFR. FENa <1% indicates prerenal physiology (kidney is still trying to conserve sodium). Muddy brown granular casts indicate some component of ATN has developed. In Kenya, ibuprofen is widely available OTC and often used without medical supervision — patient education is critical. Elderly patients on ACEi/ARB should be counselled about dehydration risks (hot weather, reduced intake, diarrhoea/vomiting) and to temporarily stop ACEi if acutely unwell (\"sick day rules\").",
    references: ["KDIGO Guidelines: AKI Management", "Kenya Clinical Guidelines: Acute Kidney Injury", "NICE Guidelines: AKI (CG169)"],
