    vitals: "HR: 90 bpm, BP: 145/89 mmHg, RR: 18 breaths/min, Temp: 38.4°C, SpO2: 88%",
    labs: "Creatinine 3.2 mg/dL (baseline ~0.9 mg/dL — acute rise). BUN 45 mg/dL. K+ 6.0 mEq/L. Na+ 135 mEq/L. Bicarbonate 18 mEq/L (metabolic acidosis). eGFR 18 mL/min/1.73m². Urine osmolality 380 mOsm/kg. Urine Na+ 18 mEq/L. Fractional excretion of sodium (FENa) 0.8%. Urinalysis: muddy brown granular casts (ATN pattern). BUN/Cr ratio >20:1 (prerenal component). Blood glucose normal.",
    imaging: "Renal ultrasound: normal-sized kidneys (R 10.5cm, L 10.3cm), preserved cortical thickness, no hydronephrosis, no stones. Increased echogenicity suggestive of acute change.",
    diagnosis: "Acute Kidney Injury (AKI Stage 2) — NSAID-Induced Prerenal AKI with Superimposed Acute Tubular Necrosis, in the setting of ACEi use and volume depletion",
    ddx: ["NSAID-Induced AKI", "ACEi-Induced AKI", "Acute Tubular Necrosis (ischaemic)", "Pre-renal AKI (dehydration)", "Obstructive Uropathy", "Rapidly Progressive Glomerulonephritis"],
    goals: "Restore renal function (normalise creatinine and urine output), correct hyperkalaemia and metabolic acidosis, identify and remove nephrotoxins, prevent permanent kidney damage",
    pharm: "1. IMMEDIATELY STOP Ibuprofen (NSAID — the primary offending agent). 2. STOP Lisinopril (ACEi — contributes to efferent arteriolar dilation and worsens GFR in AKI; restart only after creatinine normalises). 3. IV Fluid resuscitation: 0.9% Normal Saline 500mL bolus over 1 hour, then reassess. Repeat if still dehydrated (target urine output >0.5 mL/kg/hr). 4. Hyperkalaemia management: K+ 6.0 — if ECG changes (peaked T waves): IV Calcium Gluconate 10% 10mL over 5 minutes. Nebulised Salbutamol 10-20mg. IV Insulin 10 units + 50% Dextrose 50mL. If no ECG changes: Kayexalate 15g PO. 5. Correct metabolic acidosis: Sodium Bicarbonate 500mg TID PO (or IV if severe). 6. Avoid all nephrotoxins: no NSAIDs, no aminoglycosides, no contrast. 7. Monitor fluid balance carefully (risk of overload if oliguria persists). 8. Consider renal replacement therapy (dialysis) if: K+ refractory, severe acidosis (pH <7.1), fluid overload, or uremic symptoms.",
    nonPharm: "Strict fluid balance monitoring (hourly I&O). Daily weights. Renal diet: moderate protein (0.8 g/kg/day), low K+ (avoid oranges, bananas, tomatoes, potatoes). Avoid all NSAIDs permanently. Educate on reading medication labels (NSAIDs in OTC products like Combiflex, Brufen).",
    carePlan: "Stop Ibuprofen and Lisinopril. Start IV fluids. Manage hyperkalaemia aggressively. Monitor creatinine and K+ daily. Target urine output >0.5 mL/kg/hr. Once creatinine starts falling and urine output improving: gradual mobilisation. Nephrology review if no improvement in 48-72 hours. Avoid NSAIDs for future pain management — use paracetamol, topical NSAIDs, or low-dose codeine with caution.",
    dtps: "1. AKI caused by NSAIDs in the setting of concurrent ACEi use and volume depletion — a classic \"triple whammy\" nephrotoxic scenario. 2. Elderly patient at high risk for drug-induced AKI. 3. Requires medication reconciliation and patient education to prevent recurrence.",
    monitoring: "Creatinine and K+ daily until downtrending. Urine output hourly. Fluid balance chart. BUN and bicarbonate every 2-3 days. Once stable: creatinine weekly until back to baseline. Monitor for relapse if NSAIDs re-exposed. Renal function should recover within 1-3 weeks if AKI was purely prerenal/ATN from NSAIDs.",
    counselling: "The medications you have been taking for pain and blood pressure can combine to damage your kidneys, especially when you are not drinking enough fluids. Ibuprofen is particularly harmful to the kidneys. We need to stop it permanently. For pain, you can use paracetamol (up to 4g/day) or topical diclofenac gel (which has much less systemic absorption). Always check with a pharmacist before taking any over-the-counter pain medication — many contain ibuprofen or other NSAIDs. Drink at least 2 litres of water daily.",
    followUp: "Nephrology follow-up in 1 week. Repeat creatinine and K+ weekly until normalised. Review all medications for nephrotoxic potential. Long-term: avoid NSAIDs permanently. Ensure adequate hydration. BP management: restart ACEi only after AKI fully resolved, at a lower dose, with close monitoring.",
