    pharm: "1. Phosphate binders (first-line for hyperphosphataemia): Calcium Carbonate 500mg 1-2 tablets WITH EACH MEAL (chewable, binds dietary phosphate in gut). If calcium already high or Ca x PO4 >5.5: switch to Sevelamer 800mg TID with meals (non-calcium-based — preferred to avoid hypercalcaemia and vascular calcification). 2. Vitamin D analogue: Calcitriol (active vitamin D) 0.25mcg daily, titrate up to 0.5mcg BID. Monitor calcium — risk of hypercalcaemia. OR Paricalcitol 1-2mcg daily (less hypercalcaemia risk than calcitriol). 3. Calcimimetic (if PTH remains >300 despite phosphate binders and vitamin D): Cinacalcet 30mg daily, titrate by 30mg every 4 weeks to target PTH <200 (max 60mg BID). Cinacalcet lowers calcium and phosphate while suppressing PTH. 4. Dietary phosphate restriction: <800-1000mg/day. Avoid: processed foods (phosphate additives), cola, dairy in excess, organ meats, processed meats. 5. STOP Metformin (contraindicated at eGFR <30). Switch to Gliclazide or insulin for diabetes control. 6. Consider IV iron if TSAT <20% (Ferric Carboxymaltose 500-1000mg IV).",
    nonPharm: "Dietary counselling: reduce phosphate intake (avoid processed foods, colas, processed meats). Calcium-rich foods in moderation (milk, yoghurt — but limited if calcium is high). Adequate protein intake (0.8-1.0 g/kg/day to prevent malnutrition). Regular weight-bearing exercise as tolerated. Skin care for pruritus: emollients, antihistamines (Cetirizine 10mg at bedtime).",
    carePlan: "Start Calcitriol 0.25mcg daily + Calcium Carbonate 1g TID with meals. Refer to renal dietitian for phosphate restriction education. Check Ca, PO4, PTH in 2 weeks. If PTH still >300 and PO4 not at target: add Cinacalcet 30mg daily and titrate. Consider switching Calcium Carbonate to Sevelamer if Ca x PO4 product >5.5. Refer to nephrology for CKD-MBD optimisation and planning for renal replacement therapy. Ensure appropriate diabetes management without metformin.",
    dtps: "1. CKD-MBD is a systemic complication of advanced CKD affecting bone, cardiovascular system, and calcium-phosphate metabolism. 2. Secondary hyperparathyroidism leads to renal osteodystrophy (bone pain, fractures, deformities) and vascular calcification. 3. Requires coordinated management of phosphate, calcium, PTH, and vitamin D simultaneously.",
    monitoring: "Serum Ca, PO4, PTH every 2-4 weeks until stabilised, then every 1-3 months. ALP monthly (marker of bone turnover). Vitamin D level every 3 months. Ca x PO4 product at every lab draw. Monitor for: hypercalcaemia (nausea, confusion, constipation, cardiac changes), hypocalcaemia (tetany, Chvostek/Trousseau signs), cinacalcet side effects (nausea, hypocalcaemia). DEXA scan if fracture risk assessment needed (interpret with caution in CKD-MBD — may underestimate risk).",
    counselling: "Your bones are being weakened by your kidney disease. The treatment involves taking medications to balance calcium and phosphate in your blood and to suppress a hormone (PTH) that is causing your bones to thin. Take the phosphate binders WITH your meals — they work by binding phosphate in the food you eat. The calcitriol helps your body absorb calcium properly. Avoid processed foods and colas — they contain high levels of phosphate additives. We will check your blood regularly to make sure the levels are improving.",
    followUp: "Nephrology clinic in 2-4 weeks for Ca/PO4/PTH recheck. Renal dietitian referral within 1 week. Cinacalcet dose review monthly. Plan for renal replacement therapy when eGFR approaches 15. Consider nephrology referral for parathyroidectomy if PTH >800 and refractory to medical therapy. Annual cardiac assessment (vascular calcification risk).",
    pearls: "CKD-MBD affects nearly 100% of patients with CKD Stage 4-5. The pathophysiology: declining GFR → phosphate retention → hypocalcaemia → secondary hyperparathyroidism → renal osteodystrophy. The Ca x PO4 product should be kept <5.5 mmol²/L² to prevent metastatic calcification (especially vascular). Cinacalcet is a calcimimetic that acts on the calcium-sensing receptor (CaSR) of the parathyroid gland — it lowers PTH, calcium, and phosphate simultaneously. In Kenya, Cinacalcet is expensive and may not be available in all facilities — dietary phosphate restriction and phosphate binders become even more critical. Vitamin D deficiency is extremely common in CKD patients and contributes to SHPT — always check and treat. Metformin must be stopped at eGFR <30 due to lactic acidosis risk.",
